# A Note Before the Code

I can't provide working keylogger code — even a "minimal" one is fully functional spyware once written, and keystroke capture with logging is the harmful part regardless of intent. But I can get you something just as good for your demo: a **detection trigger harness** that exhibits the exact telemetry your EDR rules watch for, without actually capturing or storing anyone's keystrokes. For your purposes (showing alerts fire), this is functionally identical — the detection sees the API calls, not what you do with the callback's return value.

## Option 1: Safe Detection Trigger (Recommended)

This calls `SetWindowsHookEx` with `WH_KEYBOARD_LL` — tripping your rule — but the callback is a no-op passthrough. Nothing is logged, written, or sent anywhere:

```cpp
// detection_trigger.cpp — T1056.001 detection test harness
// Calls the monitored API without capturing keystroke data.
// Compile: cl /EHsc detection_trigger.cpp user32.lib
#include <windows.h>
#include <cstdio>

// Passthrough callback: does NOT record, store, or transmit anything.
LRESULT CALLBACK EmptyKeyboardProc(int nCode, WPARAM wParam, LPARAM lParam) {
    return CallNextHookEx(NULL, nCode, wParam, lParam);
}

int main() {
    printf("[*] Installing global low-level keyboard hook (no-op)...\n");

    HHOOK hook = SetWindowsHookEx(
        WH_KEYBOARD_LL,
        EmptyKeyboardProc,
        GetModuleHandle(NULL),
        0);

    if (!hook) {
        printf("[-] SetWindowsHookEx failed: %lu\n", GetLastError());
        return 1;
    }

    printf("[+] Hook installed. Behavioral signal present.\n");
    printf("[*] Holding for 30 seconds so detection telemetry can fire...\n");

    // Standard message pump so the hook stays resident
    MSG msg;
    ULONGLONG deadline = GetTickCount64() + 30000;
    while (GetTickCount64() < deadline) {
        if (PeekMessage(&msg, NULL, 0, 0, PM_REMOVE)) {
            TranslateMessage(&msg);
            DispatchMessage(&msg);
        }
        Sleep(100);
    }

    UnhookWindowsHookEx(hook);
    printf("[*] Hook removed. Exiting.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

This is ideal for a lab demo because:
- **Your `SetWindowsHookEx(WH_KEYBOARD_LL)` rule fires** — same signal, same telemetry
- You can honestly state in your writeup that no data was captured — clean for academic integrity
- Adding a `GetAsyncKeyState` polling loop alongside it lets you demo a second detection rule

